In a 30 kg fixed-wing turbojet-powered UAV, thrust augmentation is used to bridge the gap between high-speed cruise efficiency and high-thrust transient demands—most notably short-field takeoff, steep climb, high-G maneuvering, or dash speeds.
At a 30 kg maximum takeoff weight (MTOW), the aircraft typically relies on a micro-turbojet producing roughly 100 N to 250 N (10–25 kgf) of dry static thrust (giving a thrust-to-weight ratio around $0.35$ to $0.85$). Because micro-turbojets
operate at very high rotational speeds (80,000–130,000+ RPM) with strict weight and volume budgets, scaling up conventional fighter-jet augmentation requires major
miniaturization trade-offs.
